From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Fri, 17 Jul 2026 15:22:48 +0200
Subject: Treat context-result closure params as method-level in level checks
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A method whose result type stacks context functions gets its body
elaborated as a chain of nested closures, and erasure flattens those
closures' parameters into the method's own parameter list
(ContextFunctionResults). Capture checking, however, level-checked
references bound inside the chain against the closures' own anonfun
symbols, so the inner layer's body could not reference the outer
layer's parameter: the method-result fresh rejected it:

    Reference `contextual$1` is not included in the allowed capture
    set {any} of an enclosing function literal ...
    where: any is a root capability in the result type of method f

for e.g.

    def f(): (A^) ?=> (B^) ?=> Int = ... summon[A] ...

while the equivalent single-layer method compiles. CheckCaptures now
registers the closures counted by ContextFunctionResults
.contextResultCount when rechecking a method, and acceptsLevelOf maps
a reference whose level owner is such a closure up to the method —
aligning level checking with the post-erasure reality.

The neg test erased-methods2.scala (whose header notes it 'was a neg
test before') encodes exactly this limitation and now compiles; its
foo9a/foo10a mirror the safer-exceptions stacked-CanThrow pattern that
is sound under erasure flattening.

Proscala-Patch: ctxresult
